<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>reading-and-comprehension</title><style>table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{border:1px solid #333;padding:6px}th{background:#eee}img{max-width:100%}</style></head><body><h1>reading-and-comprehension</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Reading and Comprehension Skills</li><li>Reading Comprehension</li><li>Short Story and Poetry</li><li>Drama and Novel</li><li>Short Story 1 — The Selfish Giant (Oscar Wilde)</li><li>Short Story 2 — The Oval Portrait (Edgar Allan Poe)</li><li>Note-Making and Summary Writing</li><li>Introduction to Short Stories</li><li>Reading Comprehension: Passage Analysis</li></ul><h2>Reading and Comprehension Skills</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is Reading Comprehension?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Reading comprehension is the ability to understand, interpret, and analyse written text. It involves more than decoding words — it requires extracting meaning, making inferences, evaluating arguments, and connecting the text to prior knowledge. In the NEB Class 11 English examination, comprehension questions typically carry 10-15 marks and include unseen passages with objective and subjective questions. Success in this section depends on systematic reading strategies and practice.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Types of Comprehension Questions</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>NEB comprehension questions generally fall into four categories: (1) Factual questions — ask for information explicitly stated in the passage (e.g., &#x27;What did the protagonist decide?&#x27;). (2) Inferential questions — ask for meaning that is implied but not directly stated (e.g., &#x27;Why did the character feel trapped?&#x27;). (3) Vocabulary-in-context questions — ask for the meaning of a word as used in the passage (e.g., &#x27;What does the word &quot;desolate&quot; mean in line 5?&#x27;). (4) Interpretive/evaluative questions — ask for the reader&#x27;s understanding of the passage&#x27;s theme, tone, or author&#x27;s purpose (e.g., &#x27;What is the main idea of the passage?&#x27;). Each type requires a different reading strategy.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Strategy 1: Skimming</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Skimming is reading quickly to get the general idea or gist of a passage. To skim effectively: (1) Read the title (if given) — it often hints at the topic. (2) Read the first and last sentences of each paragraph — these often contain the main idea. (3) Read the first and last paragraphs of the passage — these usually contain the thesis and conclusion. (4) Note key words — names, dates, technical terms, and repeated ideas. Skimming should take 1-2 minutes for a standard passage. It helps you understand the overall structure before diving into details, which is essential for answering inferential and interpretive questions.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Strategy 2: Scanning</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Scanning is reading quickly to find specific information (a name, date, fact) without reading every word. To scan effectively: (1) Know what you are looking for before you start. (2) Move your eyes rapidly across the text, looking for keywords or numbers. (3) Stop when you find the relevant information and read that sentence carefully. Scanning is particularly useful for factual questions (e.g., &#x27;What year was the bridge built?&#x27;). It is different from skimming — skimming gets the gist; scanning finds a specific detail. Practising both strategies significantly improves reading speed and accuracy in exams.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Strategy 3: Close Reading</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Close reading is a careful, detailed reading of the passage to understand its deeper meaning, structure, and language. After skimming for the general idea, close reading involves: (1) Identifying the main idea of each paragraph. (2) Noting key terms and their definitions. (3) Analysing literary devices (metaphor, simile, imagery). (4) Understanding the author&#x27;s tone and attitude . (5) Making inferences about implicit meanings. Close reading takes 5-8 minutes for a standard passage but is essential for answering inferential and interpretive questions accurately.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Understanding Vocabulary in Context</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>When asked to explain a word&#x27;s meaning in context, do not simply give the dictionary definition — explain how the word functions in the specific passage. Use these techniques: (1) Read the surrounding sentences — context clues often reveal the meaning. (2) Look for synonyms or antonyms in nearby text. (3) Identify the word&#x27;s part of speech — is it a noun, verb, adjective? (4) Consider the tone — is the word used positively or negatively? For example, if the passage describes a &#x27;desolate landscape&#x27;, the word &#x27;desolate&#x27; likely means &#x27;empty, bleak, barren&#x27; — not just &#x27;deserted&#x27;. Context-based vocabulary questions are common in NEB exams and require careful attention to surrounding text.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Skimming vs. Scanning: Skimming is reading for the general idea/gist (fast, broad). Scanning is reading for specific information (fast, targeted). Skimming answers &#x27;what is this passage about?&#x27;; scanning answers &#x27;when did the event happen?&#x27; or &#x27;who said this?&#x27;. Students often confuse these strategies, spending too much time on one when the other is needed.</td></tr><tr><td>Inference vs. Prediction: An inference is a conclusion about what is already in the text (past or present). A prediction is a guess about what will happen next (future). &#x27;The character looked at the dark sky and gathered her umbrella&#x27; — inference: she expects rain. Prediction: she will get wet anyway. Inferences must be supported by text evidence; predictions are educated guesses about future events.</td></tr><tr><td>Main idea vs. Topic: The topic is what the passage is about (one word or phrase: &#x27;environmental pollution&#x27;). The main idea is what the author says about the topic (a complete sentence: &#x27;Environmental pollution is a growing crisis that requires immediate government action&#x27;). The topic is the subject; the main idea is the author&#x27;s point about that subject. NEB exams sometimes ask for either, and students confuse the two.</td></tr><tr><td>Author&#x27;s purpose vs. Author&#x27;s tone: The purpose is why the author wrote the passage (to inform, to persuade, to entertain, to describe). The tone is the author&#x27;s attitude toward the subject (formal, ironic, optimistic, critical). Purpose answers &#x27;why?&#x27;; tone answers &#x27;how does the author feel?&#x27;. A passage can have the purpose of informing with a critical tone, or the purpose of persuading with an optimistic tone.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Reading Comprehension</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Skimming and scanning.** Skimming reads quickly for the main idea; scanning searches the passage for a specific fact such as a date, name or number.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Main idea and support.** The main idea is the point of the whole passage; supporting details explain, prove or illustrate it and usually answer factual questions.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Inference.** An inferred answer is not stated outright — it combines stated details with the reader&#x27;s reasoning to reveal what the writer implies.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Vocabulary in context.** Unknown words are decoded from surrounding clues: synonyms, contrast words (but, however), examples and word roots.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Answering strategy.** Read the questions first, locate the relevant paragraph, answer in your own words unless quoting is required, and check that every part of the question is answered.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Reading Comprehension</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat &quot;Reading Comprehension&quot; as one blob → ✅ split into: reading comprehension.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Reading Comprehension (Reading and Comprehension, English, class-11-notes) covers Reading Comprehension. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Short Story and Poetry</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Short story elements.** A short story works with plot, character, setting, theme and conflict; the plot moves from exposition through rising action and climax to resolution.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Narrative voice.** First-person narration uses &#x27;I&#x27; and limits knowledge to the narrator; third-person narration can be limited to one character or omniscient.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Poetic form.** Poems are built from lines and stanzas with patterns of rhyme and rhythm (metre); free verse drops these fixed patterns.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Figurative language.** Metaphor and simile compare, personification gives human traits, alliteration repeats opening sounds, and imagery appeals to the senses.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Reading a poem.** Read aloud, paraphrase each stanza, identify the speaker and tone, then state the central message with evidence from the text.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Short Story</td></tr><tr><td>• Poetry</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat &quot;Short Story&quot; as one blob → ✅ split into: short story, poetry.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Short Story and Poetry (Reading and Comprehension, English, class-11-notes) covers Short Story, Poetry. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Drama and Novel</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Drama.** Drama is written to be performed: the story is carried by dialogue and stage directions, and its large units are acts and its small ones scenes.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Dramatic devices.** A soliloquy reveals a character&#x27;s private thoughts to the audience; an aside is a remark unheard by other characters; dramatic irony exploits the audience&#x27;s greater knowledge.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Tragedy and comedy.** Tragedy ends in suffering or death, often driven by a character&#x27;s flaw; comedy resolves conflict through humour and ends happily.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**The novel.** A novel is long prose fiction with sustained plot, multiple characters and developed setting; its chapters build subplots around the main storyline.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Key difference.** Drama shows action through spoken dialogue on stage, while the novel narrates action and inner thought freely on the page.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Drama</td></tr><tr><td>• Novel</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat &quot;Drama&quot; as one blob → ✅ split into: drama, novel.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Drama and Novel (Reading and Comprehension, English, class-11-notes) covers Drama, Novel. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Short Story 1 — The Selfish Giant (Oscar Wilde)</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>### 1. Summary</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>The Selfish Giant is a tale about a giant who builds a wall around his beautiful garden, keeping children out. As a result, winter perpetually reigns in his garden. When the giant finally lets the children back in, spring returns. Through the story, Wilde explores themes of selfishness, redemption, and the transformative power of love and compassion.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>### 2. Characters</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>- **The Giant**: Initially selfish, later becomes kind and selfless</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>- **The Children**: Symbolize innocence, joy, and renewal</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>- **The Little Boy**: Represents Christ-like figure; his tears bring spring back</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>### 3. Themes</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>- **Selfishness vs. Generosity**: The giant&#x27;s transformation shows that sharing brings happiness</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>- **Redemption**: Anyone can change and find salvation through love</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>- **Nature and Morality**: The garden&#x27;s seasons reflect the giant&#x27;s moral state</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>- **Childhood Innocence**: Children represent purity and spiritual wisdom</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>### 4. Key Symbols</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Plot: the giant builds a wall around the garden, winter reigns, the children are restored, spring returns and the giant becomes happy.</td></tr><tr><td>Characters: the Giant, the Children and the Little Boy.</td></tr><tr><td>Themes: selfishness versus generosity, redemption, nature and morality, and childhood innocence.</td></tr><tr><td>Symbols: the wall (separation, ego, isolation), winter (selfishness), spring (compassion), the rose tree and the little boy (divine love and Christ).</td></tr><tr><td>Moral: true happiness comes from sharing and loving others.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ The giant was always kind. ✅ He was selfish at first, then transformed.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The story is just about a garden. ✅ It&#x27;s a spiritual allegory about love and redemption.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> &#x27;The Selfish Giant&#x27; is Oscar Wilde&#x27;s fairy-tale allegory in which a giant walls off his beautiful garden and keeps the children out, so that winter takes permanent possession of the grounds. When he finally lets the children return, spring comes back, the rose tree blossoms and the giant discovers lasting happiness in sharing. Wilde uses the changing seasons to mirror the giant&#x27;s moral condition, and presents the little boy as a Christ-like figure whose tears restore the garden. The central lessons are the redemption of the selfish through love and the idea that true happiness comes from giving.</p><hr><h2>Short Story 2 — The Oval Portrait (Edgar Allan Poe)</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>### 1. Summary</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>The Oval Portrait is a framed story within a story. A narrator lies ill in an abandoned chateau in the Apennines. He finds an oval portrait of a young woman and reads a note explaining that it was painted by a young artist who loved his bride deeply. Consumed by his art, he neglected her, and as he painted, she slowly grew pale and died — her life force transferring into the portrait.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>### 2. Narrative Structure</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>- **Frame narrative**: Story within a story</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>- **First-person narrator**: Provides objective observation</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>- **Artistic description**: The painter&#x27;s account is embedded</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>### 3. Themes</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>- **Art vs. Life**: Art can consume and destroy life</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>- **Destructive Passion**: Obsessive love becomes self-destructive</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>- **Beauty and Death**: Beauty is transient; death gives permanence</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>- **The Artist&#x27;s Sacrifice**: Creating art requires sacrifice</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>### 4. Gothic Elements</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Structure: a frame narrative (story within a story), a first-person narrator and an embedded artistic description.</td></tr><tr><td>Plot: the ill narrator in an abandoned chateau in the Apennines finds an oval portrait and reads a note about a young painter and his bride.</td></tr><tr><td>Themes: art versus life, destructive passion, beauty and death, and the artist&#x27;s sacrifice.</td></tr><tr><td>Gothic elements: the isolated chateau, stormy weather and shadows for setting; a melancholy mood with supernatural undertones; the oval frame as a symbol of life&#x27;s fragility.</td></tr><tr><td>Literary devices: foreshadowing of the tragic ending, symbolism of the painting as a vessel of life force, and irony.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ The wife died naturally. ✅ Her life force was absorbed by the painting.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The artist loved his wife. ✅ His obsession with art consumed him.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> &#x27;The Oval Portrait&#x27; by Edgar Allan Poe is a frame narrative: a narrator lying ill in an abandoned chateau in the Apennines discovers an oval portrait and reads the account left behind by the painter. A young artist who loved his bride deeply was so consumed by his art that he neglected her, and as he painted, her life was drained away into the picture until she died. Gothic elements - the isolated chateau, stormy weather, shadows and a melancholy mood - frame a meditation on art versus life, destructive passion, the transience of beauty and the artist&#x27;s sacrifice.</p><hr><h2>Note-Making and Summary Writing</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is Note-Making?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Note-making is the process of extracting key information from a passage and organising it into a structured format using headings, sub-headings, and bullet points. In the NEB Class 11 English examination, note-making is a tested skill (typically 5-8 marks) that assesses a student&#x27;s ability to identify important information, organise it logically, and present it concisely. Note-making is different from note-taking: note-making is done after reading a passage, while note-taking is done during a lecture or speech.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Format of Notes</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>A well-structured set of notes includes: (1) Title — a concise heading that captures the main topic (e.g., &#x27;THE IMPORTANCE OF READING NEWSPAPERS&#x27;). (2) Main points — the key ideas from the passage, numbered or lettered (A, B, C...). (3) Sub-points — supporting details under each main point, indented and numbered (1, 2, 3... or a, b, c...). (4) Abbreviations — standard abbreviations can be used to save space (e.g., &#x27;govt&#x27; for government, &#x27;edu&#x27; for education, &#x27;adv&#x27; for advantage, &#x27;disadv&#x27; for disadvantage). The notes should fit on one page and use a clear hierarchical structure.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Steps in Note-Making</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>To make effective notes: (1) Read the passage carefully — understand the main idea and structure before writing notes. (2) Identify the main topics — look for the central themes or arguments (usually 3-5 main points). (3) Create a title — write a concise heading that covers the entire passage. (4) Write main points — phrase each main idea in a short heading (not a full sentence). (5) Add sub-points — under each main point, add supporting details, examples, or evidence. (6) Use abbreviations — replace long words with standard abbreviations. (7) Review — check that the notes capture all key information and are logically organised. This process typically takes 10-15 minutes for a standard passage.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Summary Writing: Definition and Purpose</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>A summary is a concise restatement of a passage&#x27;s main ideas in the writer&#x27;s own words. It is shorter than the original (typically 1/3 to 1/4 the length) and includes only the essential information, omitting examples, details, and repetitions. In the NEB exam, summary writing typically carries 5-8 marks. A good summary: (1) preserves the main ideas in logical order, (2) is written in your own words (not copied from the passage), (3) is concise (usually 80-120 words for a 200-300 word passage), (4) uses third person and present tense (unless the passage is in past tense), (5) does not include personal opinions or new information.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Steps in Summary Writing</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>To write an effective summary: (1) Read the passage thoroughly to understand the main idea. (2) Identify the main points — what are the key arguments or facts? (3) Draft the summary — write the main points in your own words, following the passage&#x27;s logical order. (4) Eliminate details — remove examples, statistics, quotations, and repetitions. (5) Check length — ensure the summary is approximately 1/3 to 1/4 of the original passage. (6) Review for accuracy — ensure the summary faithfully represents the passage without adding personal interpretation. A common mistake is to copy sentences directly from the passage — this is never acceptable in a summary.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Difference Between Note-Making and Summary Writing</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>While both note-making and summary writing involve condensing information, they differ in format and purpose: Note-making is presented in a structured, hierarchical format with headings, sub-headings, and bullet points. It is a visual organisation of information. Summary writing is presented as a continuous paragraph in prose form. It is a condensed restatement of the passage&#x27;s content. Note-making tests organisational skill; summary writing tests comprehension and paraphrasing skill. Both are commonly tested in NEB exams, sometimes together from the same passage.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Note-making vs. Note-taking: Note-making is done after reading a passage, organising information into a structured format with headings and sub-headings. Note-taking is done during a lecture or speech, capturing key points in real-time. Note-making is a structured academic skill; note-taking is a practical study skill.</td></tr><tr><td>Summary vs. Abstract: A summary condenses a passage&#x27;s main ideas in the writer&#x27;s own words (1/3 to 1/4 length). An abstract is a brief summary of a research paper or report, usually including the purpose, method, and findings. In NEB exams, &#x27;summary&#x27; is the required term; &#x27;abstract&#x27; is not used. Both are concise, but an abstract has a more rigid academic structure.</td></tr><tr><td>Notes vs. Outline: Notes are presented in a hierarchical format with headings, sub-headings, and bullet points (visual structure). An outline is a linear list of main points and sub-points, usually using Roman numerals and letters (I, A, 1, a). In NEB exams, &#x27;notes&#x27; and &#x27;outlines&#x27; are sometimes used interchangeably, but the expected format is the heading/sub-heading structure.</td></tr><tr><td>Paraphrasing vs. Summarising: Paraphrasing rewrites a specific passage or sentence in your own words (same length, same detail). Summarising condenses a longer passage to its main ideas (shorter length, fewer details). Paraphrasing preserves all the information; summarising selects only the most important information.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Introduction to Short Stories</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What is a Short Story?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>A short story is a brief work of prose fiction that can normally be read in one sitting. It focuses on a single incident, character, or idea , and develops it with economy of language. Unlike a novel, a short story usually has a limited number of characters, one central conflict, and one main effect. Edgar Allan Poe famously argued that a short story should aim at a single effect — every word, every image, every detail should contribute to one unified impression.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Key Elements of a Short Story</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Plot : the sequence of events in the story — exposition, rising action, climax, falling action and resolution. Character : the people (or animals, or forces) in the story; they can be round (well developed, changing) or flat (simple, unchanging). Setting : the time and place of the action, which influences mood and conflict. Theme : the central idea or message of the story — what the story means beyond its events. Point of view : the perspective from which the story is told — first person (I), second person (you), or third person (he/she/they; limited or omniscient). Conflict : the struggle at the heart of the story — internal (man vs self) or external (man vs man, man vs nature, man vs society).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>How a Short Story is Built</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>A good short story starts with a hook that creates curiosity, moves through rising tension to a climax (the turning point), and ends with a resolution — which may be closed (everything is settled) or open (leaving the reader to decide). Writers often use foreshadowing (hints of what will come) and symbolism (objects or actions standing for larger ideas) to deepen meaning.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Short Stories in the NEB English Syllabus</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>In the NEB (Nepal Education Board) English curriculum, short stories are studied to develop skills of comprehension, analysis and interpretation. Students are expected to identify the elements above, explain the theme in their own words, relate the story to real life, and answer questions about character, setting, plot and moral values.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>The Six Universal Elements of Fiction</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Every short story, regardless of culture or period, contains six fundamental elements: (1) Character — who the story is about; (2) Setting — when and where it takes place; (3) Plot — what happens; (4) Conflict — the central problem or tension; (5) Theme — the underlying message; (6) Style/Tone — how the author tells the story. These six elements work together like instruments in an orchestra: remove one and the story loses its coherence. When analysing any short story for an exam, systematically address each of these six elements to build a complete answer.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Types of Short Story</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Short stories can be classified by their structure and intent: Fable — a short tale with animals as characters, ending with a moral (e.g., Aesop&#x27;s Fables). Parable — a short narrative that illustrates a moral or spiritual lesson (often found in religious texts). Anecdote — a brief account of an interesting or amusing incident. Vignette — a short piece that captures a mood or moment rather than telling a complete story. Sketch — a brief, impressionistic portrayal of a character or scene. Flash fiction — an extremely short story (under 1,000 words) that delivers a complete narrative arc. Understanding which type you are reading helps you interpret the author&#x27;s intentions and the story&#x27;s effect on the reader.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Short story vs. novella: A short story is typically under 7,500 words and focuses on a single incident, while a novella is between 17,500 and 40,000 words and allows for more complex plots and character development.</td></tr><tr><td>Theme vs. subject: The subject is what the story is about in one word (e.g., &#x27;war&#x27;, &#x27;love&#x27;, &#x27;jealousy&#x27;). The theme is what the story says about that subject — it is a complete statement (e.g., &#x27;War destroys the innocence of youth&#x27;). Never write a theme as a single word in an exam.</td></tr><tr><td>Plot vs. story: The story is everything that happens in the narrative world in chronological order. The plot is the specific arrangement and selection of events as presented by the author — events may be reordered, skipped, or emphasised. A story might be told out of order (flashback, flashback within flashback), but the plot is what the reader actually experiences.</td></tr><tr><td>First-person narrator vs. author: The narrator is a fictional voice inside the text — not the author. Even when a story is written in first person (&#x27;I&#x27;), the &#x27;I&#x27; is a character, not the real person who wrote the story. Confusing narrator with author leads to misinterpretation, especially with unreliable narrators.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Reading Comprehension: Passage Analysis</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Systematic Passage Analysis</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Systematic passage analysis is a structured approach to understanding and answering questions about an unseen prose passage. In the NEB Class 11 English examination, comprehension passages typically carry 10-15 marks and may include factual questions, inferential questions, vocabulary-in-context questions, and short-answer questions. A systematic approach ensures that students extract maximum information from the passage and answer all questions accurately and efficiently.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Step 1: Preview the Passage</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Before reading in detail, spend 30-60 seconds previewing: (1) Read the title (if given) — it often reveals the topic and sometimes the author&#x27;s position. (2) Skim the first paragraph — it usually introduces the topic and main idea. (3) Skim the last paragraph — it often summarises or concludes the argument. (4) Note the paragraph topics — read the first sentence of each paragraph to understand the structure. This preview takes 1-2 minutes and provides a mental framework for detailed reading.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Step 2: Active Reading</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>During active reading, engage with the text: (1) Underline key terms — names, dates, technical terms, repeated ideas. (2) Marginal notes — write brief summaries of each paragraph in the margin (2-3 words per paragraph). (3) Note transitions — pay attention to words like &#x27;however&#x27;, &#x27;therefore&#x27;, &#x27;moreover&#x27;, &#x27;nevertheless&#x27; — these signal shifts in argument or emphasis. (4) Identify the author&#x27;s stance — is the author supportive, critical, neutral, or balanced? (5) Mark difficult words — circle words you don&#x27;t know and try to infer their meaning from context. Active reading takes 3-5 minutes for a standard passage and significantly improves comprehension and retention.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Step 3: Answering Questions Strategically</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>When answering comprehension questions: (1) Read the question first — this tells you what to look for. (2) Locate the relevant section — scan the passage for keywords from the question. (3) Read the surrounding sentences — one sentence may not contain enough information. (4) Quote directly for factual questions — use the passage&#x27;s exact words where possible. (5) Paraphrase for inferential questions — express implied meaning in your own words. (6) Keep answers concise — answer only what is asked; avoid unnecessary information. (7) Check your answer — ensure it is supported by the passage, not your own opinion. This strategic approach saves time and improves accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Types of Comprehension Questions</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>NEB comprehension questions fall into these categories: Factual recall — questions asking for information directly stated in the passage (Who? What? When? Where?). Inference — questions asking for meaning that is implied but not directly stated (Why? How? What can be inferred?). Vocabulary in context — questions asking for the meaning of a word or phrase as used in the passage. Main idea — questions about the central theme or purpose of the passage. Tone and attitude — questions about the author&#x27;s feeling or perspective. Structure — questions about how the passage is organised. Application — questions asking students to apply ideas from the passage to a new situation. Different question types require different reading strategies and answer formats.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Handling Vocabulary-in-Context Questions</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>When asked to explain a word&#x27;s meaning in context: (1) Read the entire sentence containing the word. (2) Read the sentences before and after — context extends beyond the immediate sentence. (3) Look for context clues — definitions, examples, contrasts, and restatements that reveal meaning. (4) Consider the word&#x27;s root — knowing Latin or Greek roots can help (e.g., &#x27;bene&#x27; = good, so &#x27;benevolent&#x27; = good-willed). (5) Eliminate obviously wrong options — in MCQ format, eliminate answers that don&#x27;t fit the context. (6) Substitute your answer — replace the word with your meaning in the sentence to check if it makes sense. Vocabulary-in-context questions test your ability to use surrounding information to determine meaning, not just recall dictionary definitions.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Inference vs. Opinion: An inference is a logical conclusion drawn from evidence in the passage — it must be supported by the text. An opinion is a personal belief or judgment that may or may not be supported by the text. In NEB exams, inference questions require textual evidence; opinion questions ask for your personal response. Confusing these leads to answers that are either unsupported (opinion presented as inference) or redundant (stating the obvious).</td></tr><tr><td>Main idea vs. Topic sentence: The main idea is the central point of the entire passage — it may be stated explicitly in a topic sentence or implied throughout. The topic sentence is a single sentence (usually at the beginning of a paragraph) that states the paragraph&#x27;s main point. The main idea of a passage may not have a single topic sentence — it may be developed across multiple paragraphs. In NEB exams, &#x27;main idea&#x27; questions require synthesising information from the entire passage.</td></tr><tr><td>Author&#x27;s purpose vs. Author&#x27;s main idea: The author&#x27;s purpose is WHY the author wrote the passage (to inform, to persuade, to entertain, to describe). The author&#x27;s main idea is WHAT the author is saying (the central argument or message). Purpose is about function; main idea is about content. A passage may have the purpose of persuading with the main idea that &#x27;vaccination is essential for public health.&#x27; These are related but distinct concepts tested in NEB comprehension questions.</td></tr><tr><td>Literal meaning vs. Figurative meaning: Literal meaning is the direct, dictionary definition of words — what they say on the surface. Figurative meaning is the implied, non-literal meaning — what the author suggests beyond the surface. In comprehension, literal questions ask for information directly stated; figurative questions ask for implied meaning (inferences, themes, symbolic interpretations). Students often miss figurative meaning by taking language too literally.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Reading and Comprehension Skills</td><td>Reading and Comprehension Skills covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Reading Comprehension</td><td>Reading Comprehension (Reading and Comprehension, English, class-11-notes) covers Reading Comprehension. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from ex</td></tr><tr><td>3: Short Story and Poetry</td><td>Short Story and Poetry (Reading and Comprehension, English, class-11-notes) covers Short Story, Poetry. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exa</td></tr><tr><td>4: Drama and Novel</td><td>Drama and Novel (Reading and Comprehension, English, class-11-notes) covers Drama, Novel. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Short Story 1 — The Selfish Giant (Oscar Wilde)</td><td>&#x27;The Selfish Giant&#x27; is Oscar Wilde&#x27;s fairy-tale allegory in which a giant walls off his beautiful garden and keeps the children out, so that winter takes permanent possession of the grounds. When he finally lets the chil</td></tr><tr><td>6: Short Story 2 — The Oval Portrait (Edgar Allan Poe)</td><td>&#x27;The Oval Portrait&#x27; by Edgar Allan Poe is a frame narrative: a narrator lying ill in an abandoned chateau in the Apennines discovers an oval portrait and reads the account left behind by the painter. A young artist who l</td></tr><tr><td>7: Note-Making and Summary Writing</td><td>Note-Making and Summary Writing covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Introduction to Short Stories</td><td>Introduction to Short Stories covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>9: Reading Comprehension: Passage Analysis</td><td>Reading Comprehension: Passage Analysis covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Reading And Comprehension.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>Strong writing and reading use evidence purposefully: a claim should be traceable to textual or factual support, and an explanation should show how that evidence answers the task. Audience and purpose shape structure and register.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>For reading, distinguish what a passage states from inference; for writing, follow the requested genre, audience and word limits. Do not infer an author’s intention or treat a single interpretation as certain without supporting evidence.</p><h3>Open research question — How evidence supports more than one reading</h3><p>Literary interpretation is evidence-based rather than a hunt for one hidden answer. A defensible open reading offers at least two text-supported interpretations, cites exact details, and explains what complicates each; authorial intention should not be asserted without evidence.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>In “Reading And Comprehension”, which two interpretations can the exact wording support, and what detail complicates each? Record the line or passage; leave the issue open if the text does not decide it.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://writing.wisc.edu/handbook/academicwriting/closereading/">UW–Madison: close reading</a></li><li><a href="https://ies.ed.gov/ncee/wwc/practiceguide/22">WWC: teaching secondary students to write effectively</a></li><li><a href="https://www.york.ac.uk/english/about/writing-at-york/writing-resources/close-reading/">University of York: close reading and ambiguity</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>